course: dictionary
lesson: 8322
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word rocket can be a vehicle that goes into space, a firework, a fast rise, or a green salad leaf. To choose the right meaning, read the words around it. «Big rockets drop their empty stages to become lighter.» The talk of stages and space shows that rocket means the tall vehicle.
explain: Look for clues that point one way. «Fuel needs oxygen to burn, and there is no air in space, so a rocket carries its own oxygen as well as its fuel.» Fuel and space are the clues, so this rocket is the space vehicle, not the firework.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. If "rise very fast" fits, the word is working as a verb. Verbs change form: rocket, rockets, rocketed, rocketing. «Rocketed» is the past tense, so something rose very fast in the past.
explain: Some meanings depend on where you live. In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, rocket is a peppery salad leaf, but Americans call it arugula. When context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Rocket can be a noun (a rocket, rockets) or a verb (rocket, rocketed, rocketing).
fact: A rocket is a tall vehicle with powerful engines that pushes it up into space.
fact: A firework rocket shoots high into the sky and explodes in colours, and only adults should light one.
fact: To rocket means to rise or move very fast, like prices going up quickly.
fact: «A rocket throws hot gas out downwards and the gas pushes the rocket up; it does not need air to push against.»
exercise: «Big rockets drop their empty stages to become lighter.» What is a rocket in this sentence? | A fast rise in prices | *A tall vehicle that goes into space | A firework that explodes in colours | A green salad leaf
exercise: «Prices rocketed after the bad harvest.» What does rocketed mean? | Stayed exactly the same | *Rose very fast | Exploded in colours | Fell slowly
exercise: At a café in Sydney, Ella's salad has rocket on top. What is rocket here? | A firework | *A peppery salad leaf | A tall vehicle with engines | To rise very fast
test: Which sentence uses rocket as a verb? | The rocket sat on the launch pad. | Dad put rocket in the salad bowl. | *Prices rocketed in the summer. | We watched a rocket at the fair.
test: What clue tells you that this rocket is a space vehicle? «Fuel needs oxygen to burn, and there is no air in space, so a rocket carries its own oxygen as well as its fuel.» | Peppery taste | *Fuel and space | Salad on a plate | Lit up the sky
exam: Oliver reads «The rocket exploded in colours over the river.» Which meaning fits? | A tall vehicle that goes into space | *A firework that explodes in colours | To rise very fast | A peppery salad leaf
